Z||000000||saleenfiero||10-08-2000||07:55 PM||||Just out of idle curiosity, and to help decide how much horsepower I really need in this VERY competitive world.  How much horsepower would you need to beat cars like:<P>Stocker 5.0's, Camaro Lt's and LS's respectively, overcooked civics, and other cars considered fast?  Dumb question, but just wondering.  <BR>The kicker is, I've got an auto (please no lecturing on that), so is there anything different I've gotta think about?||||reg||2
Z||000001||rockcrawl||10-08-2000||08:58 PM||||Actually I think the automatic is preferable for drag racing. When's the last time you saw a pro drag car with a manual. From my experience, my automatic Fieros always seem to take off faster. An auto also eliminates driver error. It's the top end where they suffer, but how fast can you really go between stoplights anyway? I don't race, so I can't help with the horsepower recomendation, but just don't think the auto is a disadvantage when your up against a 5.0 at a stoplight.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||10-08-2000||09:29 PM||||Depends on what you mean by competative?  If you want the fastest Fiero in Canada  something in the neighborhood of 500-600 hp should do the trick.  Now try to figure out how to build (afford)the V-8 engine that will put out that much.  Some 358 race engines get up to 700 hp.  Cost is $35000.||||reg||5
